<p class="gna-lede">CPAP is still the most reliable treatment for moderate to severe obstructive sleep apnea, so for most people who struggle with it, the first step is to get the mask and settings adjusted rather than give it up. If CPAP genuinely does not work for you, there are real alternatives — custom oral appliances, positional therapy, weight loss, nasal treatment and, for carefully selected patients, jaw surgery or an implanted nerve stimulator. The right choice depends on how severe your sleep apnea is, what is narrowing your airway and what you can manage every night.</p>

<h2 id="why-cpap-first">Why CPAP remains first-line</h2>
<p>CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure) delivers a gentle stream of air through a mask that acts like a splint, holding the throat open while you sleep. When it is worn for most of the night, it controls breathing events in nearly everyone, whatever the cause. That is why sleep physicians recommend it first for moderate to severe sleep apnea, especially when there is marked daytime sleepiness, heart disease or a job that involves driving.</p>
<p>The usual problem is not effectiveness but everyday use. Many people find the mask uncomfortable, the air pressure strange or the machine inconvenient when travelling. Before switching, ask your sleep team about:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A different mask</strong> — nasal pillows, a nasal mask or a full-face mask. A good fit matters more than the brand.</li>
  <li><strong>Heated humidification</strong> to ease dryness, congestion and nosebleeds.</li>
  <li><strong>Auto-adjusting machines (APAP)</strong> and a "ramp" setting that starts at low pressure while you fall asleep.</li>
  <li><strong>Treating a blocked nose</strong>, which makes any mask harder to tolerate.</li>
  <li><strong>Gradual desensitisation</strong> — wearing the mask while awake, reading or watching television, for a few days.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most machines record how many hours you use them. Around four hours on most nights is often treated as a minimum, but symptoms and health benefits tend to improve the longer it is worn.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>CPAP is the most consistently effective treatment for moderate to severe sleep apnea; troubleshooting should come before switching.</li>
    <li>Custom mandibular advancement devices are the main non-surgical alternative and work most predictably in mild to moderate cases.</li>
    <li>Positional therapy and weight loss help selected people and support every other treatment.</li>
    <li>Nasal surgery improves breathing and CPAP comfort but rarely resolves sleep apnea on its own.</li>
    <li>Jaw surgery (MMA) and hypoglossal nerve stimulation are options for carefully selected adults with moderate to severe disease.</li>
    <li>Whatever you choose, a follow-up sleep study should confirm that it is working.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="oral-appliances">Oral appliances: mandibular advancement devices</h2>
<p>A mandibular advancement device (MAD) looks like a pair of sports mouthguards linked together. Worn at night, it holds the lower jaw — and with it the tongue — slightly forward, widening the airway behind the tongue and making it less likely to collapse. It is the most widely used alternative to CPAP.</p>
<h3>Who it suits</h3>
<p>Sleep medicine guidelines support oral appliances for adults who cannot tolerate CPAP or who prefer an alternative. They work most predictably in mild to moderate sleep apnea, in people who are not severely overweight, and when events are worse on the back. In severe sleep apnea the response is less predictable, although some patients still do well — and an appliance that is worn every night can achieve more than a CPAP machine left in the cupboard.</p>
<p>You need enough healthy teeth to anchor the device, healthy gums and reasonably comfortable jaw joints. The appliance should be custom-made from impressions or a digital scan and adjustable, so the jaw can be advanced in small steps until symptoms improve. Ready-made "boil-and-bite" devices sold online are not recommended for sleep apnea.</p>
<h3>Side effects</h3>
<p>In the first few weeks, many people notice tooth or jaw soreness in the morning, extra saliva or a dry mouth; these usually settle. Over months to years, the bite can gradually shift, so regular dental reviews are important. People with existing jaw-joint problems need careful assessment first — our guide to <a href="/blogs/what-is-tmj-disorder/">TMJ disorder</a> explains why. Once the device is adjusted, a repeat sleep study confirms whether it is controlling your breathing, because quieter snoring does not always mean the apnea has gone.</p>

<h2 id="positional-and-weight">Positional therapy, weight loss and lifestyle changes</h2>
<h3>Positional therapy</h3>
<p>In some people, breathing events happen mainly — occasionally only — when they lie on their back, because gravity lets the tongue and soft palate fall backwards. This is called positional sleep apnea, and it shows up on the sleep study report. Small vibrating devices worn on the chest or neck, or special pillows and backpacks, encourage side-sleeping. They can work well for the right person, but many people stop using them over time, and they are not enough if events also happen on the side.</p>
<h3>Weight loss</h3>
<p>Fat around the neck and tongue narrows the airway, and abdominal fat reduces lung volume. Losing around a tenth of body weight can meaningfully lower sleep apnea severity, and in some people with mild disease it is enough to make other treatment unnecessary. Supervised diet and exercise programmes, newer weight-loss medicines (some of which have been studied specifically in sleep apnea) and, for severe obesity, bariatric surgery are all options to discuss with your physician. Regaining weight usually brings symptoms back.</p>
<h3>Everyday measures</h3>
<p>Avoiding alcohol and sedative medicines in the evening, stopping smoking, keeping a regular sleep schedule and treating nasal allergies all help, whichever main treatment you use.</p>
<div class="gna-callout">
  <p><strong>Be wary of quick fixes.</strong> Mouth tape, anti-snoring sprays, chin straps and gadgets sold online have little or no evidence in sleep apnea. Some make snoring quieter without treating the blocked breathing underneath, which can delay proper care.</p>
</div>

