<h2 id="nasal-and-throat-surgery">Nasal and throat surgery</h2>
<p>A deviated septum, enlarged turbinates or nasal polyps force mouth-breathing and make CPAP masks harder to use. <strong>Septoplasty, turbinate reduction or polyp removal</strong> often improves nasal breathing, sleep quality and CPAP comfort, but on its own it rarely resolves obstructive sleep apnea. Our article on <a href="/blogs/septoplasty-vs-rhinoplasty-difference/">septoplasty vs rhinoplasty</a> explains what each operation involves.</p>
<p>In adults with very large tonsils, <strong>tonsillectomy</strong> can substantially reduce sleep apnea. Surgery on the soft palate, such as uvulopalatopharyngoplasty, has more variable results and is now used more selectively. To see exactly where the airway collapses, surgeons sometimes recommend <strong>drug-induced sleep endoscopy</strong>: a thin camera passed through the nose while you are lightly sedated to mimic sleep.</p>

<h2 id="jaw-surgery-mma">Jaw surgery: maxillomandibular advancement (MMA)</h2>
<p>Maxillomandibular advancement moves both the upper and lower jaws forward, typically by around a centimetre, using precise cuts in the bone and small titanium plates. Because the soft palate and tongue are attached to the jaws, they come forward too, enlarging the airway along its length and making it less likely to collapse. In carefully selected adults with moderate to severe sleep apnea — particularly those with a small or set-back jaw, or who cannot use CPAP — MMA is one of the most effective surgical options available. Results still vary, and some patients need CPAP or an appliance afterwards.</p>
<p>The operation is done under general anaesthesia in hospital and is planned with detailed scans and, often, <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a>. Expect a hospital stay of a few days, a liquid and then soft diet for several weeks, and a return to desk work after roughly three to six weeks. Swelling peaks in the first few days and settles gradually over weeks to months.</p>
<p>The risks need to be weighed honestly: numbness of the lower lip and chin (common at first and usually improving, but lasting in some people), bite changes that may need orthodontic adjustment, a change in facial profile, infection, problems with plates or screws, and a small chance of the jaws relapsing over time. Our in-depth guide to <a href="/blogs/maxillomandibular-advancement-for-osa/">maxillomandibular advancement for OSA</a> covers the procedure step by step.</p>

<h2 id="nerve-stimulation">Hypoglossal nerve stimulation</h2>
<p>This is an implanted device, similar in concept to a pacemaker. A small generator placed under the skin below the collarbone is connected to the hypoglossal nerve, which moves the tongue. During sleep it stimulates the nerve in time with breathing, pushing the tongue forward to keep the airway open. You switch it on with a small remote at bedtime.</p>
<p>It is intended for adults with moderate to severe sleep apnea who cannot use CPAP, who are within a set body-weight limit, and whose airway does not collapse in a particular circular pattern at the palate (checked with sleep endoscopy). It is far less widely available than CPAP or oral appliances, access in India is limited and the device is costly. Risks include those of a minor operation, tongue soreness and the need for adjustments or battery replacement over the years.</p>

<h2 id="compare-options">The options at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Most suited to</th><th>Main considerations</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>CPAP</td><td>Moderate to severe sleep apnea; first-line for most</td><td>Highly effective when worn; mask comfort and nightly use are the challenges</td></tr>
